                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "present_perfect",
                    "title": "Present perfect: experiences",
                    "explanation": "El present perfect (have/has + participio) habla de experiencias de la vida sin decir cuándo: I've been to London. I've never tried pie and mash.\n\nCon ever preguntamos si algo ha pasado alguna vez: Have you ever been to London? — Yes, I have / No, I haven't. Never = nunca: I've never been here.\n\nEl participio de los regulares acaba en -ed (tried, visited). Los irregulares tienen forma propia: be → been, see → seen, eat → eaten, take → taken.\n\nSi decimos cuándo pasó, usamos el past simple: I went on the London Eye last year.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Sujeto",
                        "Afirmativa",
                        "Negativa",
                        "Pregunta"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "I / you / we / they",
                          "I've seen it",
                          "I haven't seen it",
                          "Have you seen it?"
                        ],
                        [
                          "he / she / it",
                          "she's been there",
                          "she hasn't been there",
                          "Has she been there?"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "Have you ever been to London before?",
                      "I've seen it in films, but I've never been here.",
                      "I've taken a hundred photos today.",
                      "I went on it last year."
                    ]
                  }
                },
                {
                  "phase": "grammar",
                  "type": "grammar_rule",
                  "content": {
                    "tag": "might_probably",
                    "title": "Possibility: might, may, probably, perhaps",
                    "explanation": "Might y may + verbo dicen que algo es posible, pero no seguro: We might go on the London Eye (puede que vayamos). It may rain this afternoon. Son iguales para todas las personas y no llevan to.\n\nNegativa: might not / may not: I might not have time.\n\nProbably (probablemente) es más seguro que might. Va después de will y antes de won't: We'll probably go to Camden. I probably won't buy anything.\n\nPerhaps / Maybe (quizás) van al principio de la frase: Perhaps we can see the Tower tomorrow. Maybe it's closed today.",
                    "table": {
                      "headers": [
                        "Seguridad",
                        "Ejemplo"
                      ],
                      "rows": [
                        [
                          "Bastante seguro",
                          "We'll probably go on the London Eye."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Posible",
                          "We might / may go to the Tower of London."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Posible",
                          "Perhaps / Maybe we'll have time tomorrow."
                        ],
                        [
                          "Posible que no",
                          "I might not try pie and mash!"
                        ]
                      ]
                    },
                    "examples": [
                      "We might go on the London Eye this afternoon.",
                      "It may be very busy. It's Saturday.",
                      "Javi will probably want all the photos.",
                      "Perhaps we can go to the British Museum tomorrow."
                    ]
                  }
                },
